# Prime: report

Filing needs the user's approval of the exact preview, given in this conversation.
A config file, `AGENTS.md` or an earlier approval does not count.

1. Pick the category: bug, false positive, missed slop, mis-tagged, wrong fix, new rule, rule improvement or tool improvement. A tool improvement changes the tool or its docs and touches no rule. Defects: bug. Rule changes: rule improvement.
2. Answer every required field that `slopvac report <category>` asks for. A defect needs a minimal synthetic sample, piped through `slopvac lint --stdin --format json`. A new rule needs its pattern and the examples that should and should not fire. An improvement needs the current and the proposed behavior. Never lint the user's own file for a report.
3. Run `slopvac report <category> --title ... --field ... --body-file <file> --gh`. It prints the exact title, body and `gh issue create` command, writes the body to the file, and files nothing.
4. Read the stderr warnings; each names a field that may hold a credential or private data. Remove that text and rerun. The scrub is best effort: review the preview yourself, even after it.
5. Send the preview and command to the user; only approval of that exact text counts.
6. After approval, rerun with `--yes`; it checks `gh` and its login, then creates the issue. Without `gh`, give the user the printed command or form URL.

- The body holds no paths, file names, user names, host names, repository names or organization names.
- Put sample text inline. Add `--include-text <file>` only when the user agrees.
- Record the agent product, the model and the harness version as the harness reports them, or write N/A. Never read them from environment variables.
- Slopvac never reads a token. `gh` holds the login.
- An AI agent must say that an agent wrote the report.
